-- Version 19 → 20: proof that somebody controls a mailbox, in flight.
--
-- One new table and nothing else. LNL-76 builds the code lifecycle before either
-- of its two callers exists, so that LNL-71 (verified address changes) and
-- LNL-74 (passwordless sign-in) consume one implementation rather than each
-- growing their own.
--
-- The dual-path story is 1.sqm's and 2.sqm's: a fresh volume runs `CREATE TABLE`
-- out of EmailCodes.sq and gets this for free; a volume already at version 19
-- runs this instead. MigrationTest migrates every earlier snapshot forward and
-- compares PRAGMA output against a fresh `Schema.create`, so drift between the
-- two files fails the build rather than the volume.
--
-- ── What existing rows get: there are none, and that is the whole story ─────
--
-- Unlike 18.sqm, which had to reason about what an un-backfilled read state
-- means to a user on deploy morning, this table starts empty and means nothing
-- until somebody asks for a code. Nothing to backfill and nothing to decide.
--
-- The `users` foreign key points at a table that already exists, so ordering
-- against any other migration does not arise.
--
-- The statement below reproduces EmailCodes.sq's CREATE TABLE, minus the prose.
-- See that file for what each column and constraint is for — in particular for
-- why `purpose` is part of the UNIQUE key, which is the one thing here that
-- would otherwise look like over-specification.

CREATE TABLE email_codes (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,

    address TEXT NOT NULL,

    purpose TEXT NOT NULL,

    code_hash TEXT NOT NULL,

    attempts INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,

    created_at INTEGER NOT NULL,

    expires_at INTEGER NOT NULL,

    user_id INTEGER,

    UNIQUE (address, purpose),

    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE
);
